#!/usr/bin/sh
# Create the development account on a machine that has none.
#
# !! THIS ACCOUNT HAS NO CREDENTIAL AND IS AN ADMINISTRATOR. !!
# Anyone who can reach a logon prompt on this machine can become it, and the
# console logs in as it automatically. That is the correct posture for a live
# image -- the medium is unauthenticated, anyone holding the ISO can boot it and
# read everything on it, and a password here would be theatre rather than a
# boundary. It is the wrong posture for anything else. A production image does
# not enable the service that runs this, provisions an empty store, and has an
# administrator create the first account with `lps add`.
#
# Being passwordless is a property of the *account*, not of the console: nothing
# scopes it to one terminal, so it is reachable from any logon prompt this
# machine grows. That is the trade this file makes, deliberately.
#
# Run by peinit as a oneshot service ordered after lpsd, because `lps` talks to
# lpsd over /run/lpsd/admin.sock and the daemon has to be listening. That is
# also why this cannot be an autorun script: autoruns are Phase 1.5, before any
# service exists.
#
# It runs as SYSTEM, which `lps` accepts alongside BUILTIN\Administrators
# precisely for this case -- at first boot there is no administrator yet, which
# is the problem this script exists to solve.
#
# No self-deletion and no idempotence guard. On a live image the root is tmpfs,
# so every boot provisions a fresh store and this runs against an empty one; on
# an installed system the installer drops this file and the service alongside it
# when it commits the filesystem to disk.
set -eu

NAME=peios
# BUILTIN\Administrators. Without it the account can traverse to files but
# cannot enumerate a directory -- the tree-wide seed ACL grants administrators
# and SYSTEM, and nothing else.
#
# Named rather than written as S-1-5-32-544: lpsd resolves group names, so the
# script says what it means. A SID would still work.
GROUP=Administrators

# --no-password, not an empty password. The store refuses the second: an empty
# password still prompts and still has to be answered, so having both would mean
# two spellings of "type nothing" that behave differently.
#
# Nothing is piped in, because nothing is asked for. --no-prompt is belt and
# braces: `lps add` asks for nothing when stdin is not a terminal, but a prompt
# appearing here would hang the boot, so the intent is stated rather than
# inferred.
#
# An absolute path: a service's environment is not a login shell's and carries
# no PATH to rely on.
/usr/bin/lps add "$NAME" --group "$GROUP" --no-password --no-prompt
